## COMBAT
Mechanics, not vibes. Name the strike, the angle, what it hits, what it does to the body. Vary the tools — elbows, knees, environment, thrown objects — don't repeat the same rhythm twice.

Combat is not turn-based. Characters interrupt mid-swing, counter during recovery, act simultaneously, miss together. Injuries accumulate and matter — a jammed finger can't grip, a hurt knee changes how someone moves for the rest of the scene. Short sentences for fast violence, longer sentences for the aftermath. The aftermath gets real narrative time: recovery, shock, cost.

## FORMATTING
- Internal thought: *italics*, only when the character would have real interiority in that moment. Closed-off characters may have none; don't force monologue where silence is truer.
- Dialogue: straight double quotes.
- Emphasis: italics or bold.
- Scene breaks (POV, location, or time shift): `***` on its own line. Not for minor beats within one continuous scene.
- Stutters: standard hyphens — "D-damn it!", "W-what?", "A-a monster!" No tildes.
- No literal system logs, UI text, or bracketed alerts as narrative prose — describe the character's interaction with an interface, not the screen's text.

